To design a keyboard interface that actively teaches the user their ancestral script while they type in the dominant language, we can create an extension for the given AI configuration. Here's a proposed design for this extension:

```json
{
  "name": "Ancestral Script Keyboard",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "An extension that teaches users their ancestral script while they type in the dominant language.",
  "extensions": [
    {
      "type": "keyboard",
      "settings": [
        {
          "name": "ancestralScript",
          "type": "string",
          "description": "The ancestral script to be taught.",
          "default": ""
        },
        {
          "name": "dominantLanguage",
          "type": "string",
          "description": "The dominant language to be typed.",
          "default": "English"
        },
        {
          "name": "learningMode",
          "type": "boolean",
          "description": "Whether to enable the learning mode.",
          "default": true
        }
      ],
      "functions": [
        {
          "name": "initialize",
          "description": "Initializes the extension.",
          "fn": function(settings) {
            this.ancestralScript = settings.ancestralScript;
            this.dominantLanguage = settings.dominantLanguage;
            this.learningMode = settings.learningMode;
            this.keyboard = new Keyboard();
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "renderKeyboards",
          "description": "Renders the ancestral script and dominant language keyboards.",
          "fn": function() {
            this.keyboard.renderAncestralScriptKeyboard(this.ancestralScript);
            this.keyboard.renderDominantLanguageKeyboard(this.dominantLanguage);
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "handleKeyPress",
          "description": "Handles key press events.",
          "fn": function(event) {
            if (this.learningMode) {
              const ancestralScriptCharacter = this.keyboard.getAncestralScriptCharacter(event.key);
              if (ancestralScriptCharacter) {
                this.emitEvent("ancestralScriptCharacterTyped", { character: ancestralScriptCharacter });
              }
            }
            this.keyboard.handleKeyPress(event, this.dominantLanguage);
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "handleCharacterTyped",
          "description": "Handles character typing events.",
          "fn": function(event) {
            this.emitEvent("characterTyped", { character: event.character });
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

This extension uses a `Keyboard` class to render both the ancestral script keyboard and the dominant language keyboard. When a key is pressed, the extension checks if the learning mode is enabled. If so, it looks up the corresponding ancestral script character for the key press and emits an event with that character. The dominant language keyboard handles the key press as usual.

To use this extension, you would need to create a `Keyboard` class that can render and handle key presses for both the ancestral script and the dominant language. The implementation of this class is beyond the scope of this answer, but it should be possible using HTML5's `KeyboardEvent` and CSS.

Once the `Keyboard` class is implemented, you can create an instance of the extension with the ancestral script and dominant language specified, and enable the learning mode if desired. The extension will then render both keyboards and teach the user their ancestral script as they type in the dominant language.
